  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Aqib Ahmad Shergojri Aged 28 vs ) Union Territory Of J&K on 4 June, 2024 Author: Javed Iqbal Wani Bench: Javed Iqbal Wani S. No. 2 IN THE HIGH COURT OF JAMMU & KASHMIR AND LADAKH AT SRINAGAR WP(Crl) No. 220/2023 Aqib Ahmad Shergojri aged 28 ... Petitioner(s) S/o Late Mohammad Shaban Shergjori R/o Padgampora Tehsil Awantipora District Pulwama, Kashmir through his uncle Ghulam Mohammad Shergojri aged 50 years S/o Abdul Samad R/o Pirpora Shopian Kashmir. Through: Mr. Shuja ul Haq, Advocate. Vs. 1) Union Territory of J&K ... Respondent(s) through Addl. Chief Secretary to Government (Home Department) J&K Sriangar. 2) District Magistrate, Pulwama Kashmir. 3) Superintendent of Police Central Jail Kotbhalwal Jammu. Through: Mr. Ilyas Nazir Laway, GA. CORAM: HON'BLE MR JUSTICE JAVED IQBAL WANI, JUDGE ORDER 04.06.2024 (ORAL)",
    "facts": "1. The petitioner in the instant petition seeks quashment of detention order No. 10/DMP/PSA/23 of 2023 dated 31.03.2023 (hereinafter for short the impugned order) passed by respondent 2 (hereinafter for short the detaining authority) under and in terms of the J&K Public Safety Act, 1978 (for short the Act of 1978) on multiple grounds. wherein the petition is being opposed fundamentally on the premise that the detention of the detenue was necessitated on account of his illegal activities prejudicial to the public order and in the process of the said detention all statutory requirements and Constitutional guarantees came to be duly fulfilled.",
    "arguments": "Counsel for the petitioner while making his submissions amongst the grounds urged in the petition pressed the ground that the activities alleged against the detenue forming basis of detention of the detenue could have been dealt with by the respondents under ordinary criminal law and recourse to preventive detention could not have been taken as a parallel measure thereto. Counsel for the petitioner would further contend that the grounds on the basis of which the detenue came to be detained by the detaining authority are ex-facie vague besides being irrelevant, as such, the detenue could not have been detained thereof. Lastly the counsel for the petitioner would contend that against the detention order, the petitioner submitted a detailed representation notwithstanding the vagueness and irrelevancy of the grounds of detention which representation was not considered by the respondents thus, vitiating the order of detention on account of infringement of right enshrined under Article 22(5) of the Constitution. 4. On the contrary counsel for the respondents while opposing the submissions of the counsel for the petitioner would contend that the detaining authority is well within its powers to detain the detenue under the preventive detention as the preventive detention is qualitatively different from punitive detention and that the preventive detention is taken recourse to in reasonable anticipation as a prejudicial measure on account of the activities of the detenue and thus recourse taken to the preventive detention is not parallel proceedings qua the criminal activities of the detenue. Counsel for the respondents would further contend that the grounds on which the detenue came to be detained are specific and relevant taken into consideration by the detaining authority on the basis of which the detaining authority derived its subjective satisfaction and consequently detained the detenue which subjective satisfaction cannot be put to judicial review of this court. Counsel for the respondents would lastly submit that the representation submitted by the petitioner post his detention came to be dealt with and considered by the detaining authority and consequently rejected and that the said representation was also considered by the advisory board and rejected and that the detenue was also heard in person by the advisory board whereafter the advisory board recommended the continuation of the detention of the detenue and consequently the detention order came to be confirmed by the competent authority. 5. Insofar as the first ground of challenge pressed by counsel for the petitioner is concerned, law is no more res-integra and stands settled in case titled as \"Haradhan Saha Vs. State of West Bengal\" reported in 1975 (3) SCC 198."
  },
